  "content": {
    "id": "p10-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "The previous exercise contained examples of nested roots of the form $\\sqrt{a+\\sqrt b}$. In this exercise we will explore these expressions more generally. Let $a$ and $b$ be positive integers, where $a^2>b$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p10-q8-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Prove the following identities:\n\n$$\\sqrt{a+\\sqrt b}=\\sqrt{\\frac{a+\\sqrt{a^2-b}}2}+\\sqrt{\\frac{a-\\sqrt{a^2-b}}2}.$$\n\n$$\\sqrt{a-\\sqrt b}=\\sqrt{\\frac{a+\\sqrt{a^2-b}}2}-\\sqrt{\\frac{a-\\sqrt{a^2-b}}2}.$$\n\nHint: start by squaring the right-hand side of each expression.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Both identities hold.\n\nSquare each right-hand side: the results are $a+\\sqrt b$ and $a-\\sqrt b$. Both right-hand sides are nonnegative, so take their positive square roots.",
          "worked": "Let $c=\\sqrt{a^2-b}$, $U=\\sqrt{\\frac{(a+c)}{2}}$ and $V=\\sqrt{\\frac{(a-c)}{2}}$. Since $0<c<a$, we have $U>V>0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}(U+V)^2&=\\frac{a+c}{2}+2\\sqrt{\\frac{(a+c)(a-c)}4}+\\frac{a-c}{2}\\\\(U+V)^2&=a+2\\sqrt{\\frac{a^2-c^2}{4}}\\\\(U+V)^2&=a+2\\sqrt{\\frac{b}{4}}\\\\(U+V)^2&=a+\\sqrt b\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $U+V>0$, this gives the first identity.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}(U-V)^2&=\\frac{a+c}{2}-2\\sqrt{\\frac{(a+c)(a-c)}4}+\\frac{a-c}{2}\\\\(U-V)^2&=a-2\\sqrt{\\frac{a^2-c^2}{4}}\\\\(U-V)^2&=a-2\\sqrt{\\frac{b}{4}}\\\\(U-V)^2&=a-\\sqrt b\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $U-V>0$, this gives the second identity.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 100
      },
      {
        "id": "p10-q8-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Suppose that $a^2-b$ is a perfect square. Prove that $\\sqrt{a+\\sqrt b}$ can be written in the form $\\sqrt x+\\sqrt y$ and $\\sqrt{a-\\sqrt b}$ can be written in the form $\\sqrt x-\\sqrt y$ for some pair of rational numbers $x$ and $y$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=\\dfrac{a+c}{2},\\quad y=\\dfrac{a-c}{2}$, where $c=\\sqrt{a^2-b}$ is an integer.\n\nThese radicands are rational; substitute them into part (a).",
          "worked": "Write $a^2-b=c^2$ for a positive integer $c$. Since $b>0$, $c<a$. Set $x=\\frac{(a+c)}{2}$ and $y=\\frac{(a-c)}{2}$; both are positive rational numbers.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sqrt{a+\\sqrt b}&=\\sqrt{\\frac{a+c}{2}}+\\sqrt{\\frac{a-c}{2}}\\\\\\sqrt{a+\\sqrt b}&=\\sqrt x+\\sqrt y\\end{align*}$$\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sqrt{a-\\sqrt b}&=\\sqrt{\\frac{a+c}{2}}-\\sqrt{\\frac{a-c}{2}}\\\\\\sqrt{a-\\sqrt b}&=\\sqrt x-\\sqrt y\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 54
      },
      {
        "id": "p10-q8-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Use your answer to part (b) to express $\\sqrt{2+\\sqrt3}$ and $\\sqrt{2-\\sqrt3}$ without nested roots.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\sqrt{2+\\sqrt3}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt6+\\sqrt2}{2}$ and $\\sqrt{2-\\sqrt3}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt6-\\sqrt2}{2}$.\n\nUse $2^2-3=1^2$ in part (b).",
          "worked": "Here $a=2$, $b=3$ and $c=1$, since $2^2-3=1^2$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sqrt{2+\\sqrt3}&=\\sqrt{\\frac{2+1}{2}}+\\sqrt{\\frac{2-1}{2}}\\\\\\sqrt{2+\\sqrt3}&=\\sqrt{\\frac32}+\\sqrt{\\frac12}\\\\\\sqrt{2+\\sqrt3}&=\\frac{\\sqrt6+\\sqrt2}{2}\\end{align*}$$\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sqrt{2-\\sqrt3}&=\\sqrt{\\frac{2+1}{2}}-\\sqrt{\\frac{2-1}{2}}\\\\\\sqrt{2-\\sqrt3}&=\\sqrt{\\frac32}-\\sqrt{\\frac12}\\\\\\sqrt{2-\\sqrt3}&=\\frac{\\sqrt6-\\sqrt2}{2}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 66
      }
    ]
  },
